laughter  we all have these kind of things that  happen to us and  we can get through them you know we can  get through it and  and transform it and uh and use it  trauma and tragedy according to these  comics are not merely things to laugh at  even if it feels good rather they are  the raw materials that comedians can use  to craft entirely new narratives instead  of merely shredding apart outdated ones  you know if you're an artist who has a  chance to say something that might  actually connect with people or help  people  you don't take that chance  what good are you this approach to  comedy is distinctly nietzsche  mark wheats contends that in his later  work the philosopher made a kind of  uneasy peace with laughter  by challenging his readers to will a new  kind of laughter  one which leaves us both amused and  philosophically elevated  to put it another way nietzsche believed  that a constructive self-affirming  laughter was  possible but had yet to be realized in  his own time  he challenged his readers to invent a  new form of laughter that would support  heroic striving in the face of adversity  rather than denigrating such efforts as  being pointless and silly  and we'd argue that the stand-up comic  who uses their struggles with mental  health as material  is helping to make that new laughter a  reality when a comic crafts a good story  about their struggles  they aren't just entertaining they're  also creating a heroic narrative  that shows them facing off against the  real challenges  of being human and in the case of mental  health that means the difficulty of  being completely reliant  on a blob of electrified goo that  frequently malfunctions  in other words they're responding to  nietzsche's challenge  by generating laughter that brings  audiences and themselves  into direct contact with the past and  the future  rather than merely escaping into the  hilarity of the present  we can see this in the work of sarah  silverman like the other comedians  interviewed in laughing matters  she cites her difficult childhood as a  sort of origin story  for a comedy career in this way she  takes a direct look at her past  rather than running from it a hundred  percent of comedians  become comedians because somewhere in  their childhood  they needed to be funny in order to  survive  her refusal to use laughter as a way of  covering up past traumas  pops up again when silverman describes  what happened to the doctor  who prescribed her xanax as a teenager  the  psychiatrist who originally put me on it  hung himself  i mean i can't just skate by that it's  crazy notice how silverman laughs and  smiles while telling the story  but refuses to dismiss her psychiatrist  suicide  as something inconsequential she can  acknowledge the irony in the situation  and even chuckle at it without denying  the traumatic effect that these events  had on her younger self  what's more she explains precisely what  that effect was  drawing a narrative arc from her past  through her present  and into the future these years of  torture and shame  kind of became my superpower silverman  is literally framing her comedy career  in terms of a superhero's origin story  far from avoiding her problems  silverman embraces them as a key part of  a creative process  without romanticizing them as the sole  source of her funniness  depression doesn't make a joke out of  silverman silverman makes jokes out of  depression  she demonstrates that comedians can  transform mental health struggles  from a taboo subject into something  worth talking about  even celebrating if only for the  duration of a set  a partner and charla for a part applies  a similar technique to the topic of  anxiety  i feel like it's weirder to not have  anxiety than to have it because i feel  like if you're not scared  you're not paying attention you know  like i feel like if you open a newspaper  today skim  maybe three headlines you're just like  seems cool he's like what  everything's on fire even the newspaper  is on fire like what are you so chill  about  here nanchala frames anxiety not as a  failing  but as the only rational response to a  society that insists we post  happy photos of ourselves on instagram  while the world crumbles around us  that's not to say that everyone needs to  get stoked on their own depression or  anxiety but it's one way to deal  comics can also transform trauma into  connection with other comics or audience  members or  insomniacs watching grainy videos on  youtube  it's one of the reasons that i love  being a comedian  because if i can find comedy people  anywhere i go in the world and i have  i know i'm going to hear good stories  and i'm going to be sitting around with  people who are  also if not okay with being up  at least perhaps more honest about it  than other folks might be  and what's more nietzschean and  transforming trauma into purpose  by using their darkest moments to  generate laughter these comics aren't  just constructing their own heroic  narratives  they're offering us the chance to create  ours too in other words  if this guy can use humor to overcome  his mental health struggles  then maybe there's hope for us and our  friends and our cranky co-workers  the real mission is if your kid is sick  or your cousin is suffering or the guy  on the other side of the cubicle wall  shows up and you never know which person  you're gonna get  how can i make those people understand  that that person's not being a [ __ ]  that day  and that person is not being  melodramatic and it's not because  they're going through a phase  it's because they're sick by making  comedy intended to keep us alive and  striving for something greater these  comedians are generating the kind of  laughter which nietzsche might be damn  proud of  a laughter that serves to construct  rather than to tear down  and that's nothing to laugh at nah just  kidding that's pretty funny  i don't want to be out in the wasteland  fat and depressed like that  there's not going to be any prozac after  the collapse but what do you guys think  can comedy help us all become better  people or should comedians just stick to  smashing watermelons  shout out to gallagher let us know in  the comments and since we're on the  subject  if you or anyone you know was going  through a hard time yo please reach out  and ask for help  mostly because you deserve it inherently  but also because  y'all mean a lot to us and if you ever  need it you can find the link and phone  number for the national suicide  prevention lifeline  right here in the description below big  thanks to our patrons for all your  support  but be sure to subscribe and ring that  bell and as always thanks for watching  man  [Music]  peace  [Music]  you
 

